The tracked Go probe, wrapper, C++ loader code, type declaration, test assertion, permission, and build configuration are project-local probe code, not an adaptation patch against NetBird, WireGuard, Go, or another fixed upstream; the upstream adaptation patch count remains 0.
This result directly confirms that the measured API 24 x86_64 application loader rejects this exact Go 1.25.12 Linux c-shared ELF at initial-exec TLS relocation. It does not establish that every HarmonyOS loader, arm64 device, Go version, alternate link strategy, or native process model has the same behavior.
No NetBird, TUN, VPN, DNS, public network, UIAbility, physical-device, signing, channel, or product-support path was exercised.
The next gate is an explicit architecture decision: either produce a separately justified loader/process/link strategy that removes the initial-exec TLS conflict and rerun this same incremental probe, or treat the in-process x86_64 Linux Go c-shared route as blocked; no runtime, netpoll, NetBird, or VPN stage may be credited before loader acceptance.
